ementation has different behavior for `--check` mode without additional flags. Let me look at what the actual expected output should be when checking a file with both valid and invalid entries. In GNU coreutils, `--check` silently ignores unparseable lines unless `--warn` is used, so if gb2sum can't parse the \"emptyFile\" hash line, it would skip it and only validate the valid line, resulting in exit 0. The hash format should be parseable though, unless gb2sum is validating hash length against the algorithm—but BLAKE2b supports variable lengths so it probably accepts any hex string. I should just fix the two test issues directly rather than debug this further.\n\nFor the tag-overrides-text issue, I need to track whether `-t` was explicitly set and reject it when `--tag` is also specified. For the exit-1-check-mixed case, I'll run the reference gb2sum binary to see what actually happens with the mixed checksum file. Both the reference and Go implementations are producing empty output for the `--check` mode, which shouldn't happen—they should be printing verification results. I'm suspecting the issue might be with how the test is constructing the file paths in the checksum file, since they reference files in the temp directory but the paths might not be resolving correctly when the check runs. Let me trace through what gb2sum actually does to understand the expected behavior better.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1965,"cache_read_input_tokens":38543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1965},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54760630-b718-4908-ba54-5ff141e754bb","uuid":"940261a3-6d8a-4637-bad2-6ee0f62de3c8"}
